Draw progress bands on the framebuffer, through every mapping the boot uses.

On a board with no serial port nothing the kernel does before PE_create_console
is visible, and that is a long way in. Until then a failure is a frozen screen
and nothing else -- made worse by start_first_cpu setting VBAR_EL1 to
LowExceptionVectorBase almost immediately, which catches an early fault in a
spin loop, so the machine does not even reset to say that something went wrong.

The hard part is that the framebuffer has to be reached three different ways
over the course of the boot, because arm_vm_init retires one mapping after
another underneath it:

  1. physically, while the V=P bootstrap mapping in TTBR0 is live -- up to
     set_mmu_ttb(invalid_ttep);
  2. through phystokv(), while TTBR1 still holds the bootstrap tables built by
     start.s -- up to set_mmu_ttb_alternate(cpu_ttep);
  3. through the physical aperture, once arm_vm_physmap_init() has built it.
     The framebuffer is a carveout immediately above the memory the kernel
     manages, so it is not in the aperture by default; this patch gives it a
     slot of its own there. The VM never sees those pages -- its free list
     comes from avail_start..avail_end, which is untouched -- so all this adds
     is a mapping. ml_io_map() would also work, but only from the end of
     arm_vm_init onwards, and it is itself a piece of machinery under test.

So the address lives in j3_fb_addr and arm_vm_init moves it along at each of
those points, and every instant of the boot from the first instruction to the
kernel's own console can be marked.

(2) also requires both bootstrap mappings in start.s to be widened, not just
the V=P one. The framebuffer is a carveout immediately above the memory the
kernel manages, so it falls outside a mapping that covers exactly memSize
bytes; eight more L2 entries bring it back in. They are spare slots in table
pages that are allocated anyway, and the whole set is abandoned at
set_mmu_ttb_alternate.

The bands are drawn in two groups so that neither grows long enough to be
miscounted from a photograph -- which is how they are read on a board like
this. The lower group runs while the kernel is still building its address
space:

    0  green    first instruction of start_first_cpu
    1  white    about to call arm_vm_init()
    2  red      the first "Unsupported memory configuration" panic was cleared
    3  magenta  arm_vm_prot_init() returned
    4  cyan     everything is built; about to retire the V=P mapping

and the upper group (row 700, clear of the booter banner) runs from there to
the kernel's own console, with pmap_bootstrap() opened up because that is
where it currently stops:

    0  green    arm_vm_physmap_init() returned
    1  white    survived set_mmu_ttb_alternate(); the kernel is on its own
                page tables
    2  red      pmap_bootstrap: kernel_pmap set up, pmap_compute_io_rgns()
                returned
    3  magenta  pmap_bootstrap: the bookkeeping tables are carved out of
                avail_start and zeroed -- the first use of the physical
                aperture by something other than this instrument
    4  cyan     pmap_bootstrap: ptd_bootstrap and pmap_cpu_data_array_init
                returned
    5  orange   pmap_bootstrap() returned
    6  yellow   arm_vm_init() returned
    7  blue     printf_init, panic_init, PE_consistent_debug_inherit and
                PE_init_kprintf are done; about to call PE_create_console()

Upper blue is the end of the instrument: PE_create_console() clears the screen
and the kernel starts printing for itself, so a panel still showing bands means
it did not get that far.

Two bands from an earlier round are gone. They sat inside
"#if defined(KERNEL_INTEGRITY_KTRR)", which this board does not define, so they
were never compiled and only confused the numbering.

The pixels have to be flushed by hand for (1) and (2), because those mappings
are write-back; the flush is left in place for (3), where the mapping is device
memory and it does nothing.

x6 through x9 are the only registers the assembly macro touches, and x0, which
holds the boot_args pointer at slot 0, is deliberately left alone. The macro
follows this file's own conventions -- .macro with positional $0/$1 and
.endmacro -- rather than the GNU spellings, which the Apple assembler this is
built with does not use.

The helpers live in osfmk/arm/arm_init.c and are not static so that
osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c can use the same code and the same geometry. The
geometry constants describe this board's display; another board needs those
changed and nothing else.

This is a debugging aid, not a fix. Drop it once the kernel reaches its own
console.

--- a/osfmk/arm64/start.s
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/start.s
@@ -85,6 +85,47 @@
